\section{Introduction}
Nuclear radiation levels are critical for monitoring radiation exposure and characterizing sources. This guide outlines the extraction procedures used in nuclear physics and radiochemistry laboratories.

\section{Core Components}
A typical nuclear level sensor consists of:
- A crystal or semiconductor (typically silicon)
- A thin film (often cadmium selenide, cadmium sulfide, or cadmium telluride)
- A light source (often xenon or argon)
- A detector array (photomultiplier tubes or CCD arrays)

\section{Calibration Procedure**}
To convert raw counts to activity, we use the Curie-Weiss law: $A = kT \ln \frac{N}{N_0}$. For a standard silicon sensor:
- Set up the array with known reference activity (usually Cs-137)
- Measure the nominal activity of your sample with a spectro-photometer
- Apply known weights (often 100 mg for a 100-cm³ sample)
- Calculate the error using the difference between reference and sample

\section{Handling Verification**}
After extraction, verify the counts against:
- The nominal activity of the reference
- The count rate at the detector
- The stability of the sample
- Any drift in the source

\section{Safety Note**}
Radiation detectors emit ionizing particles (electrons, gamma rays). Cell phones and laptops are usually not suitable detectors (thermal ionization only). Always use lead shielding.

\section{Appendix: Reference Sources**}
- National Bureau of Standards (NBS) - "Radiation Detectors and Measurement"
- American Association of Physicists - "Nuclear Physics"
- Department of Energy - "Nuclear and Radiological Protection"

