\section{Introduction}
An enhanced radiator (ER) is a device designed to amplify incoming electromagnetic radiation while minimizing internal reflection losses. This configuration is typically implemented in microwave and millimeter-wave frequency bands where the goal is maximizing gain, particularly in the millimeter-wave range where gain can be maximized around 100–300 MHz due to higher refractive index of air. Typical implementations include the parabolic dish, parabolic reflector, and array elements made from conductive structures.

\section{Component Analysis}
To analyze the performance of an ER, we first define the physical elements and their operating characteristics.

\begin{itemize}
    \item **Beam width**: The width of the waveguide element determines the effective aperture. A wider beam reduces aperture losses but increases overall system length.
    \item **Width ratio**: This defines the ratio of the beam diameter to the core diameter. Higher ratios generally improve gain but increase losses due to geometric diffraction.
    \item **Beam width variation**: The beam width should be varied continuously to monitor the gain distribution across the spectrum.
    \item **Flux coupling**: The coupling efficiency determines how much gain can be extracted before excessive power absorption occurs.
    \item **Reflector material**: The material determines the surface quality and losses. Metals like copper or aluminum are common, but ceramics or polymers may be preferred for higher efficiency and compactness.
    \item **Flux coupling**: This determines how much power is transferred from the incident radiation to the cavity interior. Higher coupling increases efficiency but increases losses.
    \item **Output coupling**: The output coupling determines how much power exits the system. Higher coupling is useful for balancing power requirements with performance goals.
    \item **Inspection**: Regular inspection of these elements is essential to ensure they meet design specifications.
\end{itemize}

\section{Analysis & Design}
The design objective for an ER is straightforward: maximize gain while minimizing losses. The key variables are the physical dimensions, the material properties chosen, and the operating conditions like temperature and frequency.

